Generally, biomolecule sensors are classified by the presence of a biorecognition element and a transducer. The biological recognition element can be selected from a variety of molecules such as enzymes, antibodies, nucleic acids, carbohydrates, and cellular structures, which interact with a specific analyte and cause some appropriate biological/ chemical changes (Figure 1). The transducer reads and converts these biological/chemical changes into a colorimetric, electrical, or other readable signal. These requirements of transducers are best addressed by porphyrins and other related macrocycles, and thus porphyrinoids can be utilized in fabricating simple, accurate, and reliable biosensor devices for rapid detection of biochemical cues to readily handle healthbased emergency scenarios, where speed of response does make a real difference. Porphyrinoids offer unique properties such as structural versatility, high stability, an aromatic $18~\pi\cdot$ - electronic structure, intense visible absorption bands, longlived excited states, chemical functionalization tunability, and a molecular dipole moment, which make them exceptional candidates for biosensing applications. It is also interesting to consider that porphyrins are known to mimic many biological functions such as optical changes, catalytic activation, and
