reversible formation of chelate complexes and undergo photophysical and/or redox changes mediated by target analytes. Nature offers much more magnificent examples of porphyrin macrocycles with potential coordination and photophysical features; for example, this molecular framework can activate and transport molecular oxygen in the biological system and it can harness sunlight for plant photosynthetic systems. As a result, to build the desired macrocycle, one can select from a large substituent library depending on the application.
In addition, porphyrin systems can be mutually combined with other organic/inorganic scaffolds to offer synergistic hybrid materials, which often lead to enhanced sensing performances to transduce the porphyrin−analyte interaction in a measurable signal. Typically, their combination provides enhanced chemical and thermal stability and ease/possibility of further modification. Moreover, while incorporating properties of both the components, the hybrid material can exhibit significantly improved or even entirely new properties while retaining the characteristics of individual systems. Other than sensing, such materials have also been used with great success in several other areas including heterogeneous catalysis, bactericidal coatings, and fuel cell components.7−11 Therefore, the organic−inorganic hybrid systems with different combinations of the two components having synergistic or complementary behaviors have seen a rise in scientific interest in the past few decades.12
Hybrid materials can be synthesized by combining free-base porphyrins or their metal complexes with the other functional scaffolds such as graphene, carbon nanotubes (CNTs), metal− organic frameworks (MOFs), quantum dots, metallic nanoparticles (MNPs), polymers, and hydrogels. Stable covalent bonds or noncovalent interactions of various types, including electrostatic interactions, van der Waals forces, hydrophobic− hydrophobic contacts, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions, metal coordination, and hydrogen bonding are what propel the synthesis. This review illuminates the chemical design of organic−inorganic porphyrinoids employed as active compounds in the detection of a diverse range of biomolecules such as dopamine, glucose, oxygen, reactive oxygen species, amino acids, nucleic acids, and proteins.
## MOLECULAR STRUCTURE
Porphyrins consist of a highly conjugated tetrapyrrolic system formed by the linkage of four pyrrole rings through methene $\scriptstyle(-\mathrm{HC=})$ bridges. There are three distinct types of carbon atoms in the porphyrin structure, $\mathrm{{C_{a}}}$ ( $\alpha$ -positions), ${\mathrm{C}}_{\mathrm{b}}$ ( $\operatorname{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\beta~}}$ - positions), and methine-bridged meso positions $\left(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ . The functionalization of these different positions, as well as plenty of possible metal coordination reactions to the inner core, provides abundant molecular design possibilities with specifically tuned chemical, optical, electrochemical, biological, and catalytic properties. Moreover, integrating porphyrin molecules with other organic/inorganic moieties by covalent linkage, noncovalent attachment, self-assembly, grafting, or intercalation offers hybrid systems with multiple functionalities and new characteristics as a result of blending distinctly different components.13−16 It is without a doubt that the combination usually retains the individual functionality, in addition to the introduction of new and unique properties. These integrations of organic−inorganic moieties lead to the construction of novel nanostructured assemblies, organic−inorganic frameworks, and nanocomposites with desired characteristics. The stringent structure−activity relationships allow tuning the porphyrin properties. Indeed, several examples of porphyrin-based hybrids with variety of substrates such as magnetic nanoparticles,17−19 nanocarbons (fullerenes, carbon nanotubes, graphene),20,21 quantum dots,22 or metal oxides23,24 have been widely represented in material chemistry. Usually tetrapyrroles with a coordinatively unsaturated central metal ion (such as $Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+},{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+})$ and nitrogen-based ligands (pyridine/imidazole) are employed for such purposes.25−28 It has been demonstrated that, in comparison to their native counterparts, such organic−inorganic hybrid systems show better biocompatibility, stability, and catalytic activity, large specific surface area, and other characteristics that render them more appropriate candidates for sensing.
Herein, based on detailed literature investigations, we present a general classification of porphyrin-based materials and their applications in the field of biomolecule sensing. We believe that the present review would provide valuable information to researchers working on designing advanced functional sensing materials. Therefore, the focus of the review is to identify the structural arrangements in porphyrinoidbased organic−inorganic frameworks and their implications toward specific biomolecules while indicating possible future developments in the field.
## CLASSIFICATION OF PORPHYRINOID-BASEDORGANIC−INORGANIC FRAMEWORKS
Depending upon the functionalization, porphyrin-based organic−inorganic frameworks can be classified mainly into the following categories as described in Figure 2: (i)
peripherally functionalized porphyrins, (ii) porphyrin-based nanoparticles, (iii) porphyrin and graphene/carbon nanotube conjugates, (iv) porphyrin and metal−organic frameworks, (v) porphyrin organic frameworks, (vi) porphyrin−metallic nanoparticle composites, and (vii) other porphyrin nanocomposites. Remarkably, all types have been intensively used for the development of biosensing tools. However, the type/molecular design of the system is determined by the requirements of the analytical task and type of analyte to be detected.
## PERIPHERALLY FUNCTIONALIZED PORPHYRINS
The peripherally functionalized porphyrinoids have provided the possibility of sensing biogenic molecules considering their ability toward recognition of a variety of analytes. Porphyrins usually possess a large potential window, on average $2.2~\mathrm{V}_{;}$ , between oxidation and reduction potentials,29,30 and the oxidation potential is close to that of the biogenic amines and can be easily tuned by peripheral functionalization, which makes them favorable to design porphyrin-based electrodes. They can be easily attached to the electrode (carbon paste, glassy carbon, carbon nanopowder, etc.) via peripheral carboxyl or hydroxyl groups during surface treatment.31,32 Moreover, they can also be designed/modified to have some solubility/dispersibility in water which allows the use of their acidic or basic water solution as electrolyte. Additionally, the free base porphyrin dications (produced in acidic electrolyte solutions) have a strong attraction for the molecules that donate electrons, lowering their oxidation potentials or enhancing the transfer of electrons to the electrode surface.33
Ana-Maria Iordache et al. reported an electrolyte with an electrode-specific chemical modification that starts the electrochemical oxidation of biogenic amines in a potential window below $\begin{array}{r}{{1.0}\ \mathrm{V}.}\end{array}$ 34 For the purpose of investigating histamine sensing, they proposed a novel asymmetrical substituted mesophenyl porphyrin, 5-(4-carboxyphenyl)-10,15,20-triphenylporphyrin (CPTPP), thin films of which were deposited on a screen-printed electrode (SPE) by matrix-assisted pulsed laser evaporation (MAPLE). An aqueous trichloroacetic acid solution served as the electrolyte and an electron acceptor for the derivatization of biogenic amines. The trichloroacetic acid−histamine molecular complex displayed a high electronegativity, favorable for both improved electron transfer and CPTPP oxidation. This research demonstrated that histamine was not directly electro-oxidized, but that the trichloroacetic acid−histamine complex increased CPTPP oxidation. AFM topography demonstrated that MAPLE-deposited thin films have greater coverage on the working electrode made of carbon than those generated by drop casting, and Raman spectrometry confirmed that the chemical structure of CPTPP was conserved in those thin films.
Lin et al. synthesized a dual function trihydroxyphenyl porphyrin $\left(\mathrm{POH}_{3}\right)$ to target a basic site (AP site) in ds-DNA using the trihydroxyphenyl substituent and the tetrapyrrole macrocycle as the recognition unit (RU) and the fluorescent signal unit (SU), respectively (Figure 3).35 Despite being far from one another, the RU and SU were prototropically allosteric. It was found that an appropriate pH favors the formation of the nonfluorescent quinine/pyrrole (O-NH) conformer of $\mathrm{POH}_{3}$ . But the complementary hydrogen bonding of the RU in O-NH with the target cytosine opposite the AP site switches on the SU fluorescence through prototropic allostery toward the phenol/isopyrrole (OH-N) conformer, while the bases of thymine, guanine, and adenine totally silence this allostery, proposing a superb selectivity in single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) analysis. Such prototropic allostery was also achieved with other hydroxyl substituted patterns as well. As a result of the separation of the SU from the RU, the SU was not directly involved in the interaction with the AP site, and thus, turn-on selectivity was realized for DNA with flanking guanine, the most easily oxidized base in DNA. Moreover, other DNA structures could not bring this allosteric effect, indicating the combination of AP site design and prototropic allosteric probe finds wide applications in DNA-based sensors.
Together with the influence of functional groups attached to the periphery of the porphyrin ring, the physiochemical properties of porphyrinoids are also strongly influenced by the central metal ion coordinated to the macrocycle. A magnesium ion coordinated to the porphyrinoid rings is utilized to absorb photons in light-harvesting systems during natural photosynthetic processes, while hemoglobin contains an iron ion that is used to carry oxygen to tissues.36,37 In a significant contribution, a multilayer biosensor composed of a pair of NIR phosphorescent metalloporphyrin indicators for measuring glucose and oxygen in the subcutaneous layer was developed.38 The multilayer biosensor was made up of four layers: a diffusion barrier, a first oxygen-sensing layer combined with an enzyme layer, a second oxygen-sensing layer, and an analyteimpermeable transparent support, as shown in Figure 4. The effective separation of both emission signals required the use of a conventional $\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{(II)}$ tetrabenzoporphyrin dye, with absorption and emission maxima at 617 and $772\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and a new intramolecularly bridged $\mathrm{{Pt}(I I)}$ complex, with absorption and emission maxima at 673 and $872~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , respectively, in the second layer. Tetraphenyltetrabenzoporphyrin complexes were used as the starting point for the Scholl reaction to create this specifically created dye class. The presented optode, for the first time, allowed simultaneous glucose and oxygen measurements at a single spot, covering a dynamic range from 0 to $150\ \mathrm{hPa}$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and from 0 to $360~\mathrm{mg/}$ dL $(20~\mathrm{mM})$ glucose at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
To photoelectrochemically monitor DNA methyltransferase (MTase) activity, a water-soluble free-base tetracationic porphyrin (5,10,15,20-tetrakis( $N$ -methylpyridinium-4-yl)- porphyrin, or $\mathrm{TMPyP})$ was used.39 By using the convenient $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions and electrostatic attraction, TMPyP was adsorbable onto the surface of a 1-naphthalenesulfonate anion (NS) grafted indium tin oxide (NS-ITO) electrode to create a straightforward photoelectrode. The attached free-base tetracationic TMPyP on $\mathrm{N}S^{-}$ -ITO $\left(\mathrm{TMPyP-NS^{-}}\right.$ ITO) displayed a relatively weak photoelectrochemical response in the presence of ascorbic acid as the electron donor, while the preadsorption of single-stranded oligonucleotide (ssON) onto the surface of $\mathrm{NS^{-}}$ -ITO before TMPyP attachment (TMPyP$\mathrm{_{ssON-NS^{-}}}$ -ITO) dramatically promoted the photoelectrochemical response of TMPyP under the same conditions. The high sensitivity of the enhancement effect toward ssON preadsorption provided the basis for label-free photoelectrochemical detection of DNA methyltransferase (MTase).
Nitric oxide (NO) measurement is crucial for cancer diagnosis because it plays an important role in the regulation of cell growth and death. Due to its short lifetime of about 5 s and rapid reactions with $\mathrm{~O}_{2},$ peroxides, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -radicals, and metalloproteins, which account for both its rapid occurrence and extremely low concentrations in biological systems, its precise detection in biological samples is, nevertheless, rather challenging. Sudeshna Chandra et al. designed functionalized layers of 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-methoxyphenyl)porphyrin $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TMPP}\right)$ that can elucidate the signal transduction in vivo and ex vivo.40 An electrode system was fabricated through a close association of porphyrins and cell culture. Direct detection of NO involved oxidation on the electrode surface producing a Faradaic redox current. Preliminary experiments also indicated that NO production from cancer cells was drastically influenced by the sensor surface, thereby affecting the analytical performance of the electrode.
Another example of a 5,10,15,20-tetraphenylporphyrin system was presented by Konopińska et al. where different metalated porphyrins were investigated for hybrid protein labels in multiple detection systems.41 About 30 distinct porphyrin types were examined in early investigations to determine how they interacted with model proteins such bovine serum albumin, ovalbumin, and immunoglobulin G, which were chosen to simulate receptors or surface blocking agents. Utilizing differential pulse voltammetry, ultraviolet− visible spectrophotometry, and spectrofluorimetry, optimal conditions were examined throughout the investigation. The working electrode, solvent, and supporting electrolyte’s effects on electrochemical detection were investigated. 5,10,15,20- Tetraphenylporphyrin tin(IV) dichloride (Sn-TPP) was chosen as one of the most promising compounds in dual and triple detection systems with higher reliability of the analysis after the changes and displacements in signal intensities caused by the addition of proteins were analyzed. Here, the impact of the proteins on the Sn-TPP signals can be managed, which was a prerequisite for the conjugation with proteins. The outcomes were contrasted with those of a related study carried out for 5,10,15,20-tetraphenylporphyrin manganese(III) chloride (Mn-TPP), which is predesignated in the labeling of hybrid biomolecules.42 $\mathrm{{\bar{s}n}}$ -TPP was characterized by a detection limit and linear response of 0.75 $\mu\mathbf{M}$ and $1-500\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ which are lower than the corresponding values of $40~\mu\mathrm{M}$ and $50{-}700~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for Mn-TPP.
Noninvasive imaging and measurement of oxygen distribution is important in several areas of biomedical science; however, the methods of oxygen measurement and imaging are not developed adequately. Oxygen-dependent quenching of phosphorescence is a minimally invasive optical technique, offering good selectivity, sensitivity, and excellent temporal resolution.43,44 Metalloporphyrins such as Pt- and Pdporphyrins exhibit exceptional photophysical properties for oxygen-dependent phosphorescence quenching techniques to measure oxygen variations in tissues under different physiological conditions. Pioneered by Wilson et al., $^{45-47}$ who measured ex vivo oxygen distribution in isolated rat livers by employing a Pd-coproporphyrin probe, these porphyrin-based oxygen sensing systems have been revolutionized with several advanced sensor chemistries, instrumentations, and measurement techniques. With the purpose of tuning the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ quenching efficiency, improving the aqueous solubility, and circumventing interactions with biological molecules, the early developed unmodified metalloporphyrins were surface functionalized with various functional groups to construct well-defined dendrimers or macromolecules. Several porphyrin dendrimers such as Oxyphors R2 and G2, which are second generation Pdporphyrin dendrimers of glutamic acid, were arguably found to be better oxygen sensors in terms of aqueous solubility and photostability as compared to their unfunctionalized analogues. They were investigated to measure the oxygen pressure in rat muscles48−50 and tumor tissues.51−53 The design, properties, and advantages of dendritic porphyrin have thoroughly been reviewed by several research groups.54,55 Evans and co-workers reported the synthesis of brightly emissive Pt- and Pdporphyrins which could detect oxygen under ambient light.56 The synthesized cyclohexyl functionalized metalloporphyrin (Figure 5A) exhibited such a strong phosphorescence that it could even be visualized by the naked eye and was employed in an ex vivo porcine skin model in the mapping of transdermal oxygenation. The porphyrin was further embedded into different polymer matrices where embedment within poly(propyl methacrylate) (PPMA) resulted in a transparent and highly breathable flexible film that exhibited the highest emission brightness and highest sensitivity among other tested polymeric materials.57 In vivo studies revealed that the porphyrin−PPMA film can be used noninvasively to detect variation in tissue oxygenation in a rabbit model and can also be integrated into a wearable device for remote monitoring of transcutaneous tissue oxygenation.58,59
A portable fiber-optic device was constructed by coating the porphyrin−PPMA material on the tip of an optical fiber for intramuscular oxygen measurements.60 Results from an in vivo tourniquet porcine model showed that the device is sensitive toward the oxygen partial pressure range of $\mathrm{0{-}80\ m m H g}$ and exhibited a reproducible response to changes in intramuscular oxygen. The same group further developed a swellable hydrogel dressing which displayed traffic-light response to oxygen. Hydrogels are characterized by their analogy to extracellular matrix owing to their soft and porous skeleton together with their huge water holding capacity and biocompatibility. The fluorescein functionalized porphyrin dendrimer in combination with four-arm PEG was crosslinked with $N$ -hydroxysuccinimide−PEG cross-linker to generate a color-changing hydrogel dressing with tunable swelling capabilities as represented in Figure 5B. The red and green luminescence of Pt-porphyrin dendrimer and peripheral fluorescein conjugate exhibited a response toward oxygen and $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ , respectively. The hydrogel formulation exhibited a red-toorange-to-green “traffic light” response to oxygen which could be visualized by the naked-eye and quantified by the HSBbased photography method.
Vinogradov et al. utilized the phosphorescence quenching dendritic $\mathrm{Pt}$ -porphyrin probe in combination with a twophoton absorbing chromophore (coumarin or rhodamine) to construct a two-photon phosphorescence lifetime oxygen sensing probe. A Pt-porphyrin polyglutamate dendrimer functionalized with PEG and coumarin and optimized in terms of solubility, quantum yield, and energy transfer is shown in Figure 5C.62 In these constructs, the two-photon absorbing chromophore absorbs and transfers energy to the metalloporphyrin core via intramolecular excitation energy transfer. Similar types of synthesized two-photon phosphorescence lifetime oxygen sensing probes have been used for noninvasive 3D measurement of oxygen distribution in regions of bone marrow and cerebrum in live animals.63−67
Porphyrin dendrimers have also been developed for intracellular oxygen measurement with the ability to penetrate cellular membranes. It is highly attractive if we can nonperturbatively measure the intracellular $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ tension within deep tissues because the intracellular hypoxic environment is very complicated and can be important in understanding diseased or inflamed tissue environments. To enhance cell penetration ability for the intracellular delivery of metalloporphyrin, a cellpermeable polycation porphyrin dendrimer was synthesized by functionalized poly(amidoamine) (PAMAM) like dendritic subunits on the surface of Pt-porphyrin.68 The dendrimer was found to penetrate the multiple cell layers spontaneously without the help of cell-penetrating peptides or delivery vesicles and exhibited sensitivity toward oxygen changes.
Scientists have successfully created selective systems with two or more porphyrin units to increase selectivity for a certain class of compounds, in addition to altering the substituent groups and central metal ions. The main reason why free-base, homo-, and heterometallic bis-porphyrins are fascinating is because of how their molecular arrangement varies depending on the chemical environment. The usage of bisporphyrins has brought attention to the critical part played by central metal ions in the interaction with analytes brought on by the modification of the spatial arrangement of the bis-porphyrin. The three distinct geometries that the ethane-bridged porphyrins often adopt are syn, anti, and tweezer forms. These conformations have been well monitored by relevant UV−visible spectroscopy shifts in solutions and thin films.69−71 The syn form is characterized by the blue shift of the porphyrin Soret band, and the anti form yields a red-shifted Soret band, while the tweezers hold an intermediate position as per the exciton coupling theory. This marked spectroscopic distinction is a facile indicator for sensory applications. Bettini et al. utilized this specific spectroscopic variation of the three different ethane-bridged bis-porphyrins to develop an effective tool to monitor histidine sensing.72 They utilized Ni and $\mathtt{C u}$ complexes and free base bis-porphyrins to evaluate the role of central ions in their interaction with histidine molecules. Histidine, an important amino acid, possesses a significant role in many enzymatic processes and is reported to have decreased concentration in lung cancer patients.73−75 Therefore, the histidine level in human plasma $\left(<0.03\ \mathrm{mM}\right)$ can be utilized as a biomarker for the early diagnosis of lung cancer. Free-base, copper-based $\left(\mathrm{CuCuPo}_{2}\right)$ , and nickel-based porphyrins $\left(\mathrm{NiNiPo}_{2}\right)$ have been proposed as the active layer to detect histidine in an aqueous environment, and surface plasmon resonance (SPR) has been utilized for its detection at a nanomolar concentration (Figure 6). The Soret bands of both ${\mathrm{CuCuPo}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiNiPo}_{2}$ are influenced by the supramolecular interaction of histidine with the bisporphyrin and corresponding changes in the absorption shift were detected by means of a Langmuir−Schaefer film of $\mathrm{CuCuPo}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiNiPo}_{2}$ . The two porphyrin structures were affected by the amino acid presence in completely distinct ways; $\mathrm{NiNiPo}_{2}$ was partially closed by histidine to produce the tweezers structure, whereas ${\mathrm{CuCuPo}}_{2}$ was affected by the creation of a broadly opened anticonformation. The transduction technique used for prototype sensing was surface plasmon resonance. Histidine could be detected by ${\mathrm{CuCuPo}}_{2}$ down to nanomolar concentrations, although $\mathrm{NiNiPo}_{2}$ had a lower sensitivity (between $10^{-4}$ and $10^{-6}\dot{\bf M},$ ). This accomplishment is particularly significant since it suggests the development of a smart array of a device that can track the content of histidine, a free amino acid, in human plasma.
## PORPHYRIN-BASED NANOPARTICLES
The ability to self-assemble molecular units into nanostructured architectures while taking advantage of supramolecular chemistry has allowed exploring the so-called chemistry beyond the molecule.76−78 Such supramolecular interactions have been exploited by molecules to self-assemble giving rise to different nanostructured motifs ranging from tubes, spheres, and rods to sheets.79 Porphyrins are one of the most promising examples of the vast array of molecular systems that are appropriate for such an approach, particularly because of their planar aromatic macrocycle, which is ideal for $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions and provides a versatile platform for peripheral decoration with groups that can offer additional interaction motifs. In fact, porphyrins, metalloporphyrins, and their assemblies like, rods,80 rings,81,82 sheets,83 spheres,84 wires,85,86 and tubes87 have been explored in the past few years to afford various structural, electronic, photocatalytic, and biological characteristics.
Fan et al. constructed an ultrathin shell of about a single molecule thickness, mainly composed of iron-porphyrin monomers, by a covalent assemble strategy (Figure 7).88 The synthesized nanocapsule shell acted as a highly efficient artificial enzyme for mimicking peroxidase activity. Based on the peroxidase like activity of Fe-TPyP based nanocapsules (Fe-TPyP-NCs), a highly sensitive calorimetric sensor for glucose was fabricated. The nanocapsule acted as a highly effective peroxidase mimic with brilliant substrate affinity in the process of catalyzing the oxidation of colorless substrate, 3,3,5,5-tetramethyl-benzidine (TMB), with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ producing a blue-colored product (oxTMB), thus demonstrating the ability of system to act as highly sensitive colorimetric glucose sensor.
Horseradish peroxidase (HRP) has also been widely employed as a trace label to detect target analytes using various detection procedures in addition to conventional enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays to construct aptasensors, cytosensors, and immunosensors. The detection signal can be amplified and the detection limit for the analyte lowered by loading HRP onto a variety of nanomaterials. However, the relatively large structure and activity dependent on protein conformation of HRP limit its loading amount. As a result of their simplicity in synthesis, variable catalytic activity, and great stability under adverse conditions, artificial enzymes make ideal alternatives for genuine enzymes. Because of this, synthetic enzymes have been thoroughly researched and used in bioassays and catalysis for many year s.89−91 Through the work of Bian et al., magnetic and hollow ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanospheres functionalized with 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxylphenyl)-porphyrin $\left(\mathrm{Por-ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right.$ HSs) were created. These nanospheres were then used as a substitute for natural peroxidase.92 Owning to the synergistic effect of porphyrin and $\mathrm{ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ the Por${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ HSs exhibited enhanced peroxidase-like activity and rapidly oxidized TMB into oxTMB, which could be observed by naked eyes in minutes. Porphyrin functionalized magnetic ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ HSs were stable over a wide temperature ranging from 10 to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Moreover, when incubated in aqueous solutions of different $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values, the maximum activity of Por$\mathrm{ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ HSs exhibited no significant change. Por- $\mathrm{ZnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ HSs could rapidly catalyze the oxidation of TMB by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce a typical blue colored solution. As peroxidase mimics, these nanocomposites display several advantages over the natural enzymes and other nanozymes. Additionally, as shown by the electron spin resonance (ESR) data, hydroxyl radicals played a significant part in the peroxidase mimetic catalytic reaction due to the synergistic effect of porphyrin and ${\mathrm{ZnFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ HSs. The synthetic organic−inorganic nanocomposite for the colorimetric detection of glucose is a low-cost, straightforward, accurate, and highly selective device.
Liu et al. recently created $\mathrm{FePPOPs.SO_{3}H_{\it3}}$ , a metalloporphyrin-based porous organic polymer, to build a highly effective peroxidase-imitating nanozyme (Figure 8).93 Nanozymes are nanomaterial-based artificial enzymes that have attracted researchers’ interest owing to their unique properties in comparison to natural enzymes.94,95 FePPOPs- $S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , is well water dispersive due to its sulfonic acid side groups, and its intriguing framework is constructed by covalently linked porphyrin units which also offered stability to the polymer. $\mathrm{FePPOPs}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ displayed excellent peroxidase-like activity toward a traditional peroxidase substrate TMB to form a blue product oxTMB within only $20~\mathsf{s}$ as a result of these distinctive properties. $\mathrm{FePPOPs.SO_{3}H}$ beat ferric porphyrin monomer and ordinary $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in terms of peroxidasemimicking activity. Two visual colorimetric sensors for the ultrafast detection of glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were built based on the exceptional catalytic activity of FePPOPs- $S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ . These sensors have a wide linear range of $200{-}1500\ \mathrm{M}$ for glucose and $50-$ $1800\mathrm{~M~}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ as well as a relative low limit of detection ( $\mathbf{\widetilde{16.38\M}}$ for glucose and $26.70\mathrm{~M~}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ ). The approach outlined in this research may offer a solution for the manufacture of innovative functional materials with highefficacy peroxidase-mimicking properties by employing metalloporphyrin-based porous organic polymer with numerous integrated excellent qualities.
In 2019, Pandey et al. reported the synthesis of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -porphyrin doped polymeric nanoparticles for the efficient detection of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose.96 The oxygen sensing nature of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -porphyrin was utilized for the detection of the glucose catalytic reaction of glucose oxidase. Both $\mathrm{Pt}$ -porphyrin and glucose oxidase were co-encapsulated into poly(lactic acid) (PLA) and PLA-alginate based particles of size in the range $0.45\mathrm{-}10~\mu\mathrm{M}.$ The system was tested in simulated interstitial fluid and found to detect glucose without any interference when compared in deionized water. The sensing results showed a linear fluorescent response with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration in the range of $\boldsymbol{0{-}6}\ \mathrm{mM}$ $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf\nabla}^{\prime}R^{2}=\hat{0.992},}\end{array}$ )
and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensitivity was correlated to glucose catalytic reaction ranging from 0 to $10~\mathrm{mM}$ $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf\nabla}^{'}R^{2}=0.968^{'},}\end{array}$ ) with a response time of $4\mathrm{min}$ . In a similar study, polymer dots conjugated with glucose oxidase and doped with porphyrin were also used to detect glucose concentration.97 The glucose oxidase activity in the presence of glucose decreased the local $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration which resulted an increase in the photoluminescence quantum yield. The intensity ratio of red porphyrin to blue polymer dot increased with an increase in glucose concentration. Moreover, the polymer dots were sufficiently bright to be visualized with a smartphone device after subcutaneous injection in a mouse.
## PORPHYRIN AND GRAPHENE/CARBONNANOTUBE CONJUGATES
Surface functionalization of pristine graphene with organic molecules can transform it into chemically sensitive and soluble/dispersible material, thereby enabling its use in biosensing technology. Graphene is known to be more prone toward homogeneous, reproducible functionalization in comparison to other carbon nanomaterials. The most common strategies to functionalize graphene are as follows:
(a) Covalent functionalization onto the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms of the $\pi$ -conjugated skeleton. The functionalization can take place at the edges or at the basal plane.98−100   
(b) Heteroatom doping offers another possibility of covalent functionalization.10   
(c) Noncovalent functionalization via the adsorption of aromatic compounds based on $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and hydrophobic−hydrophobic interactions on the graphene framework.
The covalent functionalization of graphene can be achieved by nucleophilic substitution, electrophilic addition, and condensation. This can transform the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms into $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ configuration which disrupts the delocalized $\pi$ -electron cloud and thereby converts graphene into an insulator. The formation of covalent bonds offers greater stability, controllability over the degree of functionalization, and reproducibility while generally altering the native electronic and physical structure of graphene by converting $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ to $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ carbon atoms.
Noncovalent functionalization can be achieved by polymer wrapping and interaction with metal nanoparticles, porphyrinoids, biomolecules, and surfactants. This type of functionalization neither destroys the original $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon atoms nor creates $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ hybridized defects, while retaining their high electroconductivity. However, noncovalent functionalization is generally very nonspecific and offers no control over the degree of functionalization.
Researchers have utilized the noncovalent functionalization of porphyrin structures onto graphene to detect miRNA, an important biomarker for the early diagnostics of cancer,102−105 diabetes,106 and even cardiac diseases.107 Generally, its rapid detection relies on classical techniques such as Northern blot, real-time quantitative reverse transcription PCR (qRT-PCR), or optical DNA chips based SPR or fluorescence labeling. De Souza et al. reported a carboxylic acid functionalized cobalt porphyrin (CoTCPP, cobalt tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin)) noncovalently physisorbed (via $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions) on reduced graphene oxide (Figure 9A) and then immobilized on a glassy carbon electrode.108 Further, the carboxylic groups were covalently grafted with amino-terminated oligonucleotide probes, complementary to a short microRNA target. These cobalt porphyrins with carboxylic acid functionalities are known to catalyze oxygen reduction with the 2- or 4-electron transfer mechanism. 109,110 Also, the cobalt porphyrins are found to bind with nucleic acids through weak interactions with binding constants in the range of $10^{5}{-}10^{6}\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ . Thereby the electrodes reported combine the high electrical conductivity and specific area of reduced graphene oxide with the electrocatalytic properties of cobalt porphyrin toward oxygen electroreduction. Functionalization of this probe with short miRNA strands leads to a conformational change of these DNA strands, forming a randomly coiled structure to a wellorganized double-stranded architecture. This molecular reorientation influences the electrocatalytic behavior of CoTCPP in two ways: (a) weak interactions between CoTCPP and nucleic acids may be broken upon hybridization, and (b) the diffusion of oxygen, which was lowered due to randomly coiled probes, may be restored upon hybridization. This leads to amplified catalytic properties of cobalt porphyrin toward oxygen reduction, resulting in high current densities despite a very low quantity of electroactive material. Apart from oxygen, no other reagents are required and miRNA detection limit in the sub-nanomolar range was achieved.
A similar nanocomposite involving CoTCPP adsorbed on reduced graphene oxide via $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and hydrophobic interactions has been reported by Yang for the detection of uric acid.111 The biosensor showed a wide detection range of $0.02\mathrm{-}5.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ and a lowest detection limit of 1 $\mu\mathbf{M}$ with a quick response, repeatability, and stability. The larger surface area of the nanosheets enhanced the biosensor performance owing to its high conductivity and quick electron transfer between porphyrin and r-GO.
Since enzyme-linked immunosorbent tests are frequently used to create immunosensors, a novel HRP mimic trace label was created by stacking Fe-porphyrin on graphene and labeling it with streptavidin via an amidation process. The developed trace label demonstrated significantly increased peroxidase activity toward $o$ -phenylenediamine oxidization in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ improving sensitivity for electrochemical biosensing. A sensitive DNA biosensing technique was thus created by immobilizing the biotinylated molecular beacon on a modified glassy carbon electrode coated with a mixture of gold nanoparticles and single-walled carbon nanohorns $\mathrm{\langleAuNPs-}$ SWCNH).112 The molecular beacon probe was predicted to be in the “closed” state in the absence of target DNA, which prevented the streptavidin on the trace label from specifically interacting with the biotin, which was shielded due to the strong steric hindrance. On the other hand, after the molecular beacon probe hybridized with target DNA, the resulting rigid duplex was expected to unwind and move the biotin group away from the electrode surface, making the biotin ligand easily accessible to the streptavidin on the trace label. Overall, a “signal on” electrochemical sensor based on a porphyrin based HRP mimic as a trace label was constructed for highly specific and sensitive detection of target DNA, which could also distinguish target DNA from other triple-base or single-base mismatched oligonucleotides.
Wu et al. synthesized porphyrin-functionalized graphene for the sensitive and highly selective detection of dopamine.113 The anionic meso-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) used in the study was found to bind graphene through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking thereby resulting in improved solubility and stability of graphene. The anionic porphyrin−graphene system further interacted with positively charged dopamine through electrostatic and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and accelerated the electron transfer. The designed electrochemical sensor was highly selective in sensing dopamine even in the presence of ascorbic acid and uric acid. The sensor was successfully utilized for the detection of dopamine in human urine and serum samples. Zhang et al. developed a similar but slightly modified system to be used as an electrochemical aptasensor for the specific and sensitive detection of myoglobin.114 A TCPP-functionalized AuNP-conjugated graphene nanocomposite was constructed and further grafted with myoglobin-binding aptamers (MbBAs). The aptamer with ferrocene was utilized as an electrochemical probe for target binding (Figure 9B). In the presence of myoglobin, the myoglobin-binding aptamer showed a conformational change and resulted in an increase in height of attached ferrocene from the electrode surface. This resulted in decreased electron transfer rate which was directly associated with the concentration of myoglobin. These studies showed that the excellent electrochemical behavior of graphene makes it a promising electrode material for electroanalysis.
Similar to graphene, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have also been utilized to construct porphyrin nanoconjugates for biosensing applications. The metalloporphyrin decorated CNTs exhibit a sharp change in the optical and electrical properties when they encounter analytes due to various interactions such as van der Waals forces, hydrogen bonding, or electrostatic forces that can occur between them.115 Huang et al. synthesized an electrochemical sensor based on Znporphyrin and multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) which exhibited high sensitivity and stability for detecting ascorbic acid.116 Based on the synergism shown by both Znporphyrin and MWCNTs, excellent results were observed for the oxidation of ascorbic acid. The sensor displayed a linear response toward ascorbic acid at concentrations ranging from $18.72\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ to $1.85~\mathrm{mM}$ with sensitivity of $19.16~\mu\mathrm{\bar{A}~\bar{m}M^{-1}}$ . Recently, Wang et al. reported a continuous network of TCPPSn complexes (Figure 10) coated uniformly around MWCNTs through noncovalent interactions, which exhibits a good adsorption affinity for various proteins such as bovine serum albumin (BSA), cytochrome c (cyt c), lysozyme (Lys), and bovine hemoglobin (BHb).117 The zeta potential measurements showed that the adduct surface was negatively charged due to the rich carboxyl groups of the porphyrin moiety. Therefore, the adduct can interact with all the positively charged amino acids (or below the isoelectric point) via electrostatic interactions. Along with electrostatic interactions, hydrogen bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, and coordination with the metal ion could also take place, which made the adduct exhibit excellent adsorption capacity for various proteins.
## PORPHYRIN AND METAL−ORGANIC FRAMEWORKS (MOFs)
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials constructed by attaching metal ions or clusters to organic linkers through coordination bonds. They possess intriguing physiochemical properties owing to their high surface area, high thermal and mechanical stability, low density, and various functionalities which allow them to behave as high-performance sensors. They are promising substitutes for natural enzymes in biomolecular sensing and show easily measurable responses due to changes in their electrical conductivity, magnetism, ferroelectricity, luminescence, and chromism.118,119 For increasing their efficacy, MOFs are modulated via dopant addition, postsynthesis modification, and nanoparticle insertion. Owing to strong optical and electrochemical properties of porphyrins, they are embedded in the metal−organic framework giving more chemical stability, a smaller energy band gap, and extended conjugation. These enhanced properties of the adducts help in various applications such as the photoinduced electron transfer (PET) process, Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET), gas adsorption, energy storage, and conversion. Porphyrin-based MOFs have been investigated for the detection of a diverse range of biomolecules such as nucleic acids,120,121 heparin,122 dopamine,123 and enzymes,12 4−127 together with several small molecules such as hydrogen sulfide,128 hydrogen peroxide,129 and nitric oxide.130
As an example, Ling et al. synthesized a porphyrin encapsulated MOF which acts as a sensitive electrochemical biosensor based on mimetic catalysis of the MOF and the allosteric switch of a hairpin DNA.131 The functional MOFs were synthesized by one-pot encapsulation of iron(III) meso5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin chloride (FeTCPP) into a prototypal MOF, namely, HKUST-1(Cu), which was further conjugated with streptavidin (SA), the recognition unit, and was brought onto the electrode surface. The hairpin DNA was activated with the addition of target DNA which unfolds the hairpin DNA structure resulting in SA aptamer formation. The SA aptamer thus formed selectively combines with SA present on the FeTCPP@MOF-SA electrode via biorecognition affinity which showed a measurable electrochemical signal. The highly specific nature of the biosensor was observed by its ability to discriminate even a single-base mismatched oligonucleotide which leads to its remarkable efficacy. The synthesized MOF showed detection limits at the femtomolar level with a detection range of over 6 orders of magnitude. MOF nanosheets (ZnTCPP(Fe)) have been developed by Cheng et al. as peroxidase mimicking nanozymes for in vivo monitoring of heparin activity in live rats.132 Upon interaction with heparin, a heparin-specific AG73 peptide adsorbed on the MOF nanosheets resulted in detachment from the MOF thus exposing the MOF active sites as depicted in Figure 11. The peroxidase mimicking exposed MOF surface catalyzed the oxidation of Ampliflu Red to resorufin in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and the obtained fluorescence signals of resorufin were correlated with the concentration of heparin.
Detection of dopamine in the biological system can be an important factor in understanding its function in physiological processes because it plays a crucial role in neuromodulators and neurotransmitters in the brain.133 An advanced Cu-MOFbased composite was developed as a sensitive electrochemical ratiometric sensor for dopamine detection.134 Because of its large surface area and exceptional electrical conductivity, it provided a suitable surface for dopamine electrochemical reaction. Dopamine with an oxidation potential of $0.2\mathrm{V}$ and a cationic nature in buffer solution was found to interact selectively with the prepared porphyrin-MOF sensor and caused a strong increase in the oxidation current peak, whereas neutral paracetamol as a reference molecule displayed negligible changes.
Further, a zirconium-based porphyrin MOF named as PCN222 (porous coordination network-222) was exploited as a novel photoelectrochemical sensor for label-free detection of a phosphoprotein ( $\alpha$ -casein), which exhibited excellent photocurrent response toward dopamine under the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated aqueous media.135 Three different zirconium-based porphyrin MOFs (PCN-222, PCN-223, and PCN-224) with different linker connectivity are shown in Figure 12.136 The porphyrin unit presented in the MOF was shown to transmit electrons from valence to conduction band upon light irradiation which resulted in the formation of electron−hole pairs. These holes were scavenged by many electron donor species such as dopamine to enhance photoelectrochemical responses. In addition, dopamine could oxidize to provide electrons that could occupy the MOF conduction band. The photocurrent response of the modified porphyrin-MOF showed a stronger and more stable photoelectric conversion ability in comparison with the porphyrin ligand due to decreased band gap and increased electronic transitions. The MOF electrode was further soaked in different concentrations of $\alpha$ -casein solution. As the concentration of $\alpha$ -casein increased, photocurrent produced significantly decreased due to steric hindrance caused by biological macromolecules which prevented the electron transfer between PCN-222 and dopamine. A new porous coordination network (PCN-222(Fe)) based on Feporphyrin and zirconium-based MOF was synthesized by Aghayan et al. for mimicking catalase and peroxidase enzyme activity in the biosensing of glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{O}}_{2}$ .137 Glucose was first treated with glucose oxidase for its conversion into gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The PCN-222(Fe) network then utilized the produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for the catalytic oxidation of TMB to oxTMB. The intensity of color change was directly proportional to the concentration of glucose and could be measured quantitatively. A detection limit of $2.2\mu\mathrm{M}$ of glucose was observed in the linear range from 12 to $75\ \mu\mathrm{M}.$ . The same group further extended the work and modified the PCN222(Fe) network with sodium dodecyl sulfate micelles which resulted in strong electrostatic interaction and thereby exhibited enhanced peroxidase and catalase activity for glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensing.138 The modified framework exhibited two times lower limit of detection for glucose as compared to that of PCN-222(Fe) representing a more selective and accurate biosensor. On the same line, Li et al. utilized another porous coordination network (PCN-224) and incorporated Fe(III)
ions into PCN-224 nanoparticles to synthesize $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}.224$ MOF nanoparticles which exhibited enhanced peroxidase-like activity due to their unique porous structure.139 Their catalytic activity was studied by altering $\mathsf{p H}$ and temperature, and higher catalytic rate constant of the synthesized MOF was observed as compared to other peroxidase-like nanozymes.
Real-time detection of hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ fluctuations is crucial to understand its function in human physiological processes as it is considered as a third important gaseous signaling molecule following nitric oxide and carbon monoxide. A Cu-porphyrin-based aluminum (Al) containing nano-MOF was introduced as a fluorescent probe for sensitive detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ under physiological $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ (Figure 13).140 In the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{-}}$ , $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ ion in the center of Cu-porphyrin reacted with $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , thus producing $\mathbf{CuS}$ and unmetalated free-base porphyrin. The fluorescence produced by free-base porphyrin was correlated with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concentration since Cu-porphyrin does not show any fluorescence. Furthermore, the confocal imaging demonstrated the nano-MOF’s ability to detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in HepG2 and A549 intracellularly. A novel sensing material constituted of Fe-porphyrin based MOF was developed for the intracellular detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .141 By utilizing the peroxidase mimicking nature of the Fe(III)-porphyrin and rigid porous structure of MOF which can make every porphyrin accessible to the substrate, an artificial enzyme mimic with good electrochemical activity was obtained. The system even showed potential for detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ released from living cells.
## PORPHYRIN ORGANIC FRAMEWORKS
Porphyrinoid-based covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are bestowed with a large extended $\pi$ -conjugation due to the involvement of varied substituents. They can be synthesized by reversible or irreversible pathways where reversible pathways involve reactions including Schiff base condensation, boronate ester formation, and squaraine chemistry and the irreversible pathways involve reactions such as $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ coupling and chemical oxidative polymerization reactions. These frameworks have acquired a tremendous interest in the sensing field due to their design flexibility, excellent coordination abilities, high quantum yields, high fluorescence yield, high stability, large specific surface area, and tunable pore structures. Due to extensive conjugation, porphyrinoid COFs show significant changes such as quenching on addition of external stimuli such as acid, base, metal ions, and organic functionalities.
Cui et al. reported a synthetic Sonogashira cross-coupling reaction between iron 5,10,15,20-tetrakis( $\dot{\mathbf{4}}^{\prime}$ -bromophenyl)- porphyrin and 1,3,5-triethynylbenzene to form a porphyrinbased porous organic polymer (FePPOP-1) as shown in Figure 14, which was demonstrated as a novel candidate for colorimetric determination of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ glucose, and some antioxidants.142 The synthesized polymer possesses a large Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area, high sensitivity, and stability. Glucose was detected indirectly by oxidizing it in the presence of glucose oxidase which produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and gluconic acid. The peroxidase-like mechanism followed by the COF was assessed by adding a fluorescent probe, i.e., terephthalic acid which captures the hydroxyl radicals generated. Afterward, the antioxidative capability of natural antioxidants such as ascorbic acid, gallic acid, and tannic acid on the COF was evaluated and compared. As depicted in the Figure 14C, there was a gradual increase in fluorescence intensity with an increasing FePPOP-1 concentration, suggesting that FePPOP-1 decomposed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce hydroxyl radical. The UV−visible spectra indicate that absorbance of TMB and FePPOP-1 mixture has a linear relationship with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration.
Results depicted the decrease in UV−visible absorption intensity of the $\mathrm{TMB-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{-FePPOP-}$ 1 mixture with addition of ascorbic acid, gallic acid, and tannic acid. The absorbance intensity of TMB oxidation was quenched by the addition of 55, 100, and $10~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for ascorbic acid, gallic acid, and tannic acid, respectively. Therefore, the antioxidative abilities of these antioxidants were observed to be in the order: tannic acid $>$ ascorbic acid $>$ gallic acid.
Wang et al. reported an Fe-COF nanocomposite by a postsynthetic strategy for the first time in which metalation of the COF was performed with Fe at room temperature.143 In a colorimetric sensor for the sensitive detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ it was found that the synthesized Fe-COF exhibited peroxidase-like catalytic activity and outperformed HRP in terms of stability, catalytic activity, and selectivity. The detection limit was 1.1 $\mu\mathbf{M},$ , while the detection range was between 7 and $500~\mu\mathrm{M}.$ . Additionally, the one-pot method of measuring glucose can be used in conjunction with the Fe-COF and glucose oxidase $\left(\operatorname{GOx}\right)$ , and the acquired detection range was from 5 to 350 $\mu\mathbf{M}_{,}$ ; the detection limit was $1.0~\mu\mathrm{M}$ .
## PORPHYRIN−METALLIC NANOPARTICLECOMPOSITES
Metal nanoparticles owing to their unique electronic, optical, and magnetic properties have been used for innovative research in the enzyme mimetic field. Nanocomposites of metal nanoparticles were explored due to the enhancement of activity of the metal nanoparticles mainly via synergic effect. One low-cost and accurate method to detect biomolecules is a colorimetric assay in which analytes can be observed by the naked eyes.144,145 To detect biomolecules, one of the widely utilized methods is to mimic enzyme activity.146,147 The nanocomposites were reported to show activity similar to that exhibited by the natural peroxidases and hence were successfully explored as enzyme mimics for the recognition of these substrates. Since many enzymes and proteins are known to contain HRP and heme as cofactors, composites of porphyrin have been used in a variety of biomimetic catalysis and intrinsic peroxidase catalytic activity .148−150 Composites of nanoparticles were found to show less deterioration in the original activity with each cycle as composite formation removed the probabilities of agglomeration.
A study by Liu et al. reported the functionalization of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles by 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxylphenyl)-porphyrin $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}\right)$ .151 The functionalized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocomposites improved peroxidase like activity by catalyzing TMB oxidation in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The results demonstrated the catalase-mimicking activity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ which efficiently converted $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into hydroxyl radicals. The functionalized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was recognized to detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose molecules. The detection method exploited was a selective colorimetric method with TMB following Michaelis− Menten kinetics. The lower detection limits for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose were found to be $4\times10^{-7}\mathrm{mol/L}$ and $8.6\times10^{-7}\mathrm{mol}/$ $\mathrm{L,}$ respectively. In a similar concept of catalase-mimicking activity, nanocomposites of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ were also explored and found to effectively catalyze the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into hydroxyl radicals.152 Another polymorph of magnetic iron oxide, i.e., ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3},$ a stable material having magnetism, was observed to present weak peroxidase activity, while its composite with meso-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)- porphyrin studied by Liu et al. displayed excellent selectivity and sensitivity toward glucose catalysis activity.153
Another analytic platform constructed by Liu et al. for the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose exploited $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ TCPP-NiO nanocomposites making their practical feasibility in medical diagnostics.154 The experiment was successfully performed on real samples of human serum for glucose analysis obtaining satisfactory results with high sensitivity and selectivity.
Research on the biosensing applicability of the metal nanoparticle−porphyrin based nanocomposites is not only restricted to the transition elements but has been extended to the rare earth elements as well.155,156 Nanoceria owing to its shape and dimension dependent properties was found to present significant catalase mimetic activity in $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ oxidation state. There are a few findings in the literature demonstrating the catalase activity of ceri a,157,158 while nanoceria−H2TCPP nanocomposites are found to exhibit excellent properties widening its applicability for numerous other applications as well.
Apart from sensing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose widely with the porphyrin-based sensors, a report on sensing single-stranded DNA molecules was presented by Kaur et al.159 The genosensor based on gold nanoparticle−cobalt porphyrin nanocomposites can be employed in multiplexing devices for sensing a wide range of target DNA sequences with high sensitivity and quick recognition. Recently, Yang et al. have designed an experiment where the peroxidase like catalytic activity efficiency of phosphorylated iron porphyrin-based nanocomposites is enhanced by the localized surface plasmon resonance.160 Enhancement in the activity was observed due to the synergic effect of hot electrons and photogenerated electrons from phosphorylated iron porphyrin. FagadarCosma and co-workers utilized a hybrid of gold nanoparticles and $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{III})$ tetratolylporphyrin chloride (MnTTPCl) for the detection of ascorbic acid and compared their efficacy with bare MnTTPCl and gold nanoparticles.161 The hybrid system showed very nice efficiency toward ascorbic acid with detection concentration in the range of $2.6\times10^{-6}$ to $4.38\times$ $10^{-5}$ M. A porphyrin−gold hybrid has also been utilized to detect L-cysteine, a semiessential amino acid.162 Significantly high or low levels of cysteine are associated with many health disorders such as liver damage, loss of muscles and fat, Parkinson’s and Alzheimer’s diseases, diabetes, and skin lesions thus it is important to develop a method to detect cysteine efficiently. A hybrid system containing gold nanoparticles and manganese meso-tetra(pentafluorophenyl) porphyrin developed by Bonacin and co-workers162 showed quick response toward cysteine at $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ with linear response in the range of $12.2{-}33.6~\mu\mathrm{mol/L}$ with limit of quantification and limit of detection equal to 8.15 and $2.40\ \mu\mathrm{mol/L},$ respectively. Gold nanoparticles functionalized with thrombin binding aptamer have also been utilized for the detection of thrombin, a multifunctional molecule functioning as both anticoagulant and procoagulant. Under the optimal conditions, the system displayed good thrombin detection efficiency with linear response in the range of $0.1{-}25\ \mathrm{nM}$ and lowest detection limit of 0.02 nM.163
Tu et al. employed porphyrin functionalized titanium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ nanoparticles for photoelectrochemical detection of glutathione (GSH), an important natural antioxidant which plays a significant role in modulating redox homeostasis and prevents cellular damage triggered by various reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were functionalized with iron meso-tetrakis(4-sulfonatophenyl) porphyrin chloride (FeTPPS) via bidentate binding of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with sulfonic acid groups of FeTPPS as represented in Figure 15, and the obtained nanohybrid was coated onto indium tin oxide electrode for electrochemical response.164 Under optimal conditions, the hybrid system was found to detect glutathione in the concentration range of 0.05 to $2.4\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ with lower detection limit of $0.03\mathrm{\mmol/L}$ at a signal-to-noise ratio of 3.
## OTHER PORPHYRIN NANOCOMPOSITES
Double-stranded DNA has been monitored by nanohybrid composites of quantum dots−cationic porphyrin biosensor by exploiting its turn-on−off−on fluorescence properties.165 The turn-on−off−on mechanism of the nanohybrid involves “turnon” state in fluorescence measurement, and this bright fluorescence is quenched in the presence of cationic porphyrins (“turn off” state). This process is utilized for the selective sensing of DNA strands utilizing their optical responses. This whole process is based on the electrostatic interaction between the negatively charged quantum dots, positively charged porphyrins, and negatively charged DNA strand (Figure 16A). The designed fluorescent probe can give quantitative information about the DNA as fluorescence intensity is directly proportional to the concentration of DNA molecules. DNA sensing has also been performed by designing a porphyrin−DNA complex by groove interaction and based on photoelectrochemistry.166
Photoelectrochemical sensing of carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) has been performed by Zhu et al. by constructing a linear bicontinuous donor−acceptor array of 5,15-bis(4- carboxyphenyl)10,20-bis(mesityl) porphyrin, $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BCPP}\right)_{n}\mathrm{-C}_{60},$ on an indium−tin oxide electrode.167 The high photocurrent generation capability of the $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BCPP}\right)_{n}\mathrm{-C}_{60}$ array which decreases on interaction with CEA is the footing of the biosensor. The resulting decrease in photocurrent intensity on interaction with CEA (which resulted from the competition between CEA and CEA-CdTe) is analytically examined providing information about the concentration of CEA. The detection limit $\scriptstyle\left(3.4\ p g/\mathrm{mL}\right)$ of the designed immunosensor for CEA is much lower than that of earlier explored biosensors.
A 3D carbon-based nanocomposite of porphyrin was designed by Rabiee et al. by engineering the space between a reduced graphene oxide layer with multiwalled carbon nanotubes for the incorporation of $\mathrm{CoNi}_{2}S_{4}$ .168 The presence of several active sites in the nanocomposites at graphene, CNT, porphyrins, and $\mathrm{CoNi}_{2}S_{4}$ molecules provides an innovative kind of host−guest relationship which offers potential biomedical applications. The developed nanocomposite was found to perform biosensor applicability along with gene delivery applicability due to its promising interaction with genetic material established via $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction and the donor−acceptor relationship with the ${\bf d}_{7}$ transition metal cobalt.
A biodegradable Pt-porphyrin encapsulated polymeric system was developed for fluorescence-based sensing of oxygen and glucose in interstitial fluid without any interference with high sensitivity.169 Further a study on composite hydrogels, tested for low level of oxygen for real-time bioanalyte monitoring was done by Bornhoeft et al.170 The hydrogel composite was composed of alginate microparticles acting as bioactive material embedded in alginate hydrogel along with other enzymes, optical indicator, and diffusion barrier. The developed optical bioresponsive hydrogel composite material presented high sensitivity and dynamic range for the monitoring of low and high levels of glucose at ambient and physiological oxygen levels. Zang et al. constructed a catalytic hairpin assembly (CHA)-programmed DNA−porphyrin complex as an enzyme-free photoelectrochemical biosensor for ultrasensitive detection of DNA.171 The target-assisted CHA reaction was utilized to form double-strand DNA (dsDNA) which was immobilized on an electrode containing capture DNA/CdS quantum dots (Figure 16B). The resulting dsDNA interacted with porphyrin (FeTMPyP) via groove interaction and the formed FeTMPyP@dsDNA complex catalyzed the oxidation of luminol to generate a desirable chemiluminescence signal. Thus, by integrating the signal amplification ability of CHA and in situ generation of chemiluminescence emission, an ultrasensitive photochemical DNA biosensor was procured with a detection limit at the femtomolar level and high selectivity to differentiate mismatched DNA. The same group reported another DNA biosensor utilizing a dual heminlabeled DNA probe (HLDP) as a photoelectrochemical initiator. In the presence of target DNA, the hairpin capture DNA on the electrode surface could initiate CHA recycling and generated HLDP/C-DNA as a signal probe. Hemin, as an enzyme mimic in situ catalyzed the oxidation of luminol, leading to the significant enhancement of photocurrent under amplified chemiluminescence excitation. Owing to the high photoconversion efficiency of $\mathrm{CdS}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2},$ the constructed biosensor displayed high sensitivity, selectivity, and reproducibility and showed good prospects in biodiagnosis and analysis.
Zhu et al. utilized porphyrin-functionalized titanium dioxide decorated montmorillonite nanocomposite $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-TiO}_{2}\right)\_$ MMT) for “on−off” colorimetric sensing of glutathione.172 The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-TiO}_{2}\mathrm{-MN}$ T nanocomposite was found to exhibit intrinsic peroxidase-like activity that could catalyze TMB oxidation to oxTMB in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with change of color from colorless to blue under visible light irradiation as depicted Figure 17. Addition of the glutathione was found to cause an absorbance decrease and color fading and which can even be seen by the naked eyes. An additional fluorescent probe assay demonstrated that the good performance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCCP-TiO}_{2}$ -MMT was attributed to hydroxyl $\left(\mathrm{OH^{\bullet}}\right)$ radical generated by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition which was triggered by electron−hole pairs of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCCP-TiO}_{2^{-1}}$ MMT generated upon visible light irradiation. Overall, the system displayed the excellent glutathione sensing ability with detection limit of $0.057~\mu\mathrm{M}$ with the linear detection range of $0.1{-}20~\mu\mathrm{M}$ .
## SUMMARY AND OUTLOOK
This review focused on contemporary chemical modifications in porphyrinoid-based organic−inorganic composites to be utilized as biosensing platforms. The impact of chemical modifications on the porphyrin’s physiochemical properties that serve an essential role to engineer and tune efficient biosensor systems is discussed in detail. Due to strict structure−property relationships, one can tune the porphyrin properties with a wide range of modifications, thereby expanding the possibility of sensor design. Simple colorimetric responses to $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ or ion changes, detectable by the naked eye, UV−visible, fluorescence spectroscopy, and electrochemical measurements, were evolved into elaborate imaging devices to detect/monitor the stages of certain diseases. In fact, the photosensitive nature of porphyrins is not only limited to determining certain substrates, but porphyrin-based composites can also perform efficient energy/electron transfer components in photoelectrochemical signaling for bioanalysis. Porphyrinoids and carbon nanomaterials work together synergistically to detect such photoelectrochemical signaling. Carbon nanomaterials’ one-dimensional electrical structure, high conductivity, and vast surface area make it possible for electrons to move quickly between the aromatic macrocycle and the electrode to create active layers with superior sensor capabilities.
A variety of innovative porphyrin-based composites with nanoparticles, metal−organic frameworks, carbon-based materials, polymers, and hydrogels have been summarized for biosensing applications. Depending upon the chemistry utilized, porphyrinoid composites can sense selectively biomolecules such as oxygen, histamine, dopamine, reactive oxygen species, amino acids, carbohydrates, nucleic acids, and proteins. The current scenario of the development of biosensors is focused on searching for new ways of synergistically combining porphyrinoids with nanostructured materials to enhance their sensitivity, selectivity, and stability in buffered salt solutions and biological media. Porphyrinoids have a brilliant future in this area; however, still many challenges need to be faced such as complexity of “real” samples in the field with repeatability, reliability, and robustness. Hopefully, this review would attract the attention of clinicians and scientists who are working in biosensing fields to adapt porphyrin-based technologies for the detection of various analytes. Further research in designing porphyrin-based biosensors may lead to novel bioanalytical tools and biomedical devices, especially for cancer, pathogens, and other deadly conditions.
